with $r_0 = 0.511$ MV. Figure 3 shows the analytic approximations in comparison with numerical results (crosses) for small, medium and high rigidities. For both models (DT and RS) we have very good agreement between the approximations and the numerical results for the parallel mean free path. **Fig. 5.** The parallel spatial diffusion coefficient $\kappa_{\parallel}$ for the random sweeping model. ### 5.3. The parallel spatial diffusion coefficient In this section we calculate the parallel spatial diffusion coefficient $\kappa_{\parallel}$. With the equations for the mean free path and $$
\kappa_{\parallel} = \frac{v}{3} \lambda \tag{55}
$$ we can write the parallel spatial diffusion coefficient as $$
\frac{\kappa_{\parallel}}{\kappa_0} = \frac{r}{\sqrt{r_0^2 + r^2}} \frac{\lambda}{\lambda_0} \tag{56}
$$ with $$
\kappa_0 = \frac{c\lambda_0}{3}. \tag{57}
$$ Figures 4 and 5 show $\kappa_{\parallel}/\kappa_0$ for the DT- and the RS-model, respectively. If we would adopt as in many previous studies the simple relation $\kappa_{\perp} = \alpha\kappa_{\parallel}$ with constant $\alpha$, Figs. 4 and 5 also show the rigidity dependence of the perpendicular spatial diffusion coefficient for the dynamical turbulence and random sweeping turbulence model. ## 6. The mean free path for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$ In the last section we calculated the mean free path for special heliospheric parameters. Because of the limited time resolution of plasma detectors in spacecraft measurements, it is not clear that the dissipation range spectral index $p$ is equal to 3. In this chapter we therefore calculate the mean free path for different values of the dissipative spectral index $p$. We restrict our analysis to particle rigidities smaller than $10^4$ MV, because we know <!-- page 9 -->
**Fig. 6.** Parallel mean free paths for electrons, positrons and protons in the damping model for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$. **Fig. 7.** Parallel mean free paths for electrons, positrons and protons in the random sweeping model for different values of the dissipation range spectral index $p$. that for higher rigidities the mean free paths for both damping models (DT and RS) are independent of $p$ (see Eq. (34)). With Table 3 for the DT-model and Table 4 for the RS-model it is straightforward to calculate the mean free path for different dissipation range spectral indices $p$. The results are shown in Fig. 6 for the DT-model and Fig. 7 for the RS-model. For both figures we used the parameters of Eq. (50). We notice that there is no $p$-dependence for protons in both models. For the DT-model and for electrons and positrons we only find small differences for medium rigidities. For higher rigidities there is no $p$-dependence and for small rigidities in agreement with our analysis in Sect. 4 we find that the mean free path is proportional to $(p-2)/(p-s)$. For the RS-model we find that the dependence of $p$ is very strong. For higher values of $p$ and for medium rigidities we find that the value of the mean free path becomes larger than the size of the heliosphere. The fact that the parallel mean free path becomes larger than the dimensions of the heliosphere, may actually not be a problem. Because the magnitude of the perpendicular mean free path is typically only a few percent of the parallel mean free path, the effective radial diffusion coefficient may still be small enough to justify a diffusion approximation. ## 7. Summary and conclusion The parallel mean free path of cosmic ray particles in partially turbulent electromagnetic fields is a key input parameter for cosmic ray transport. In this work we have calculated the parallel mean free path of cosmic ray particles in two particular turbulence models: slab-like dynamical and random sweeping turbulence. We extended our earlier calculations by allowing a flat energy range in the power spectrum with finite wave amplitudes at very small wavenumbers. We derive new general formulas for the mean free path (see Tables 3 and 4). These new formulas can also be used for high particle rigidities. We explicitly determine the rigidity dependence and the absolute value of the mean free path for different cosmic ray particles for the DT- and RS-model. In both models the non-zero plasma wave intensity at wavenumbers smaller than $k_{min}$ yields smaller values of the mean free path at high rigidities as compared to the power spectrum with no wave intensity below $k_{min}$ considered in TS. We also discuss the influence of the dissipation range spectral index $p$ and the asymptotic properties of the mean free path for both models. ## Appendix A: Solving the integral $D$ for the damping model of dynamical turbulence For the damping model of dynamical turbulence we must solve the integral $$
D = \int_{0}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x} \left[ \frac{1}{1 + a^2/R^2 (\mu R - x)^2} + \frac{1}{1 + a^2/R^2 (\mu R + x)^2} \right]. \quad (A.1)
$$ This integral can be solved exactly and we find that $$
D = \frac{1}{1 + a^2 \mu^2} \left\{ \frac{1}{2} \ln \left[ (1 + \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] + \frac{1}{2} \ln \left[ (1 - \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] \right. \\ \left. + a\mu \cdot \arccot \left[ (1 - \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] - a\mu \cdot \arccot \left[ (1 + \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] \right\}. \quad (A.2)
$$ While this integral can be solved exactly, for the $\mu$-integration we must employ approximations for special cases. <!-- page 10 -->
A.1. The case $\mu R \ll 1$ and $a/R \ll 1$ In the first case we automatically have $a\mu \ll 1$ and we have $$
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1 + a^2 \mu^2} &\approx 1 \\
\ln \left[ (1 \pm \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] &\approx -2 \ln (a/R) \\
\arccot \left[ (1 \pm \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] &\approx \arccot (a/R) \approx \frac{\pi}{2} \tag{A.3}
\end{align*}
$$ to find $$
D(\mu R \ll 1, a/R \ll 1) \approx -2 \ln (a/R). \tag{A.4}
$$ A.2. The case $\mu R \ll 1$ and $a/R \gg 1$ In this case we must approximate the logarithmic function with $$
\ln \left[ (1 \pm \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] \approx \pm 2\mu R + \frac{R^2}{a^2} - \mu^2 R^2 \tag{A.5}
$$ and the arccot-function with $$
\arccot \left[ (1 \pm \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] \approx \frac{R}{a} (1 \mp \mu R) \tag{A.6}
$$ to find finally $$
D(\mu R \ll 1, a/R \gg 1) \approx \frac{R^2}{a^2}. \tag{A.7}
$$ A.3. The case $\mu R \gg 1$ and $a\mu \gg 1$ In this case we can use the approximations $$
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1 + a^2 \mu^2} &\approx \frac{1}{a^2 \mu^2} \\
\ln \left[ (1 \pm \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] &\approx 2 \ln (\mu R) \\
\arccot \left[ (1 \pm \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] &\approx \arccot (\pm a\mu) \tag{A.8}
\end{align*}
$$ to find $$
D(\mu R \gg 1, a\mu \gg 1) \approx \frac{\pi}{a\mu}. \tag{A.9}
$$ A.4. The case $\mu R \gg 1$ and $a\mu \ll 1$ In this last case we automatically have $a/R \ll 1$ and we can use $$
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{1 + a^2 \mu^2} &\approx 1 \\
\ln \left[ (1 \pm \mu R)^2 + \frac{R^2}{a^2} \right] &\approx -2 \ln (a/R) \\
\arccot \left[ (1 \pm \mu R) \frac{a}{R} \right] &\approx \arccot (\pm a\mu) \approx \frac{\pi}{2} \tag{A.10}
\end{align*}
$$ to find $$
D(\mu R \gg 1, a\mu \ll 1) \approx -2 \ln (a/R). \tag{A.11}
$$ Appendix B: Solving the integral $D$ for the random sweeping model For the RS-model we must solve the integral $$
D = \int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x} \left[ e^{-b^2/R^2 [\mu R + x]^2} + e^{-b^2/R^2 [\mu R - x]^2} \right]. \tag{B.1}
$$ This integral can not be solved exactly, but we can again employ approximations for four special cases. B.1. The case $\mu R \ll 1$ and $b/R \ll 1$ In this case we can use $x \gg \mu R$ so that

where G(x, p, t) = *VUplUp* is the differential cosmic-ray density gradient. Substitution of (3.2) and (3.5) into (3.1) gives the cosmic-ray transport equation $$\frac{\partial U_p}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (\mathbf{V}U_p - \mathbf{\kappa} \cdot \nabla U_p) - \frac{1}{3} (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{V}) \frac{\partial}{\partial p} (pU_p) = 0, \qquad (3.6)$$ where we note that the last term contains contributions from both the spectral term of (3.2) and the momentum change rate (3.5). It is important to note again that all the quantities appearing in the above-mentioned equations are observed in a stationary reference flame in terms of the variables (x, p, t) specified in this stationary frame. The derivation of the transport Equation (3.6) has a history, the first derivation being given by Parker (1965) and extended by Jokipii and Parker (1970). Parker (1965, 1966) started his argument by showing that energy changes of cosmic rays in the interplanetary medium have to be taken into account. He showed that Fermi acceleration could be neglected, and argued that all cosmic-ray particles lose energy when viewed from the flame moving with the solar wind because of adiabatic deceleration as they move with the expanding IMF irregularities. The rate of change of momentum of particles due to adiabatic deceleration is (Parker 1965, Dorman 1965), $$\frac{\langle \dot{p}' \rangle_{ad}}{p'} = -\frac{\nabla \cdot \mathbf{V}}{3},\tag{3.7}$$ where p' is the particle momentum as seen by an observer moving with the solar wind. The transport equation for the differential number density U\* (x, *p', t)* is then derived (Jokipii and Parker, 1970), where x is defined in the stationary frame, but *p'* is the momentum as seen in the frame moving with the solar wind. This equation is then transformed to where the momentum is also specified in the stationary frame, and (3.6) results. According to the Jokipii-Parker derivation, the last term of (3.6) therefore contains the effects of adiabatic deceleration of cosmic rays in the solar wind. Webb (1975) however, took exception to this view. He argued that adiabatic deceleration can only be applicable in the case of convective transport, that is strong scattering where the components of the diffusion tensor ~0. The cosmic rays are then effectively constrained to move with the solar wind as they scatter between the IMF irregularities which behave like the walls of a 'magnetic box'. Consequently the particles change momentum at the adiabatic rate (3.7) within the magnetic box whose walls move with the solar wind velocity V. Webb then took a different point of view, and showed that cosmic rays with momentum p' relative to the solar wind lose momentum at a rate $$\langle \dot{p}' \rangle / p' = -\frac{1}{3} \nabla \cdot \mathbf{V}$$
 (3.8) because the solar wind reference frame is a non-inertial frame. This rate is identical to the adiabatic rate (3.7), and when transformed to the stationary frame, it results in (3.5). Therefore the difference in interpretation of cosmic-ray momentum changes by Jokipii and Parker on the one hand and Webb on the other hand does not effect the resulting transport equation. Alternative derivations of the transport equation were given by Gleeson and Axford (1967) and Dolginov and Toptygin (1967, 1968). These authors derived (3.6) from the Boltzmann equation in which the cosmic rays are scattered by hard sphere scattering centers embedded in the solar wind. In neither of these approaches adiabatic (or another form of) deceleration was postulated a priori. ## 3.2. SOLUTIONS OF THE MODULATION EQUATIONS To facilitate solutions and interpretations of (3.2) and (3.6) we assume a right-handed heliospherieal polar coordinate system (r, 0, qS) with unit vectors (er, e0, %) pointing radially outward, from the solar North Pole and in the azimuthal directions (east-west) respectively. If ~ is the angle between the radial and average magnetic field directions, the diffusion tensor (1.1), with to(, 1) = ~c~ 2) becomes $$\begin{cases}
\kappa_{rr} & \kappa_{r\theta} & \kappa_{r\phi} \\
\kappa_{\theta r} & \kappa_{\theta \theta} & \kappa_{\theta \phi}
\end{cases} = \begin{cases}
\kappa_{\parallel} \cos^{2} \psi + \kappa_{\perp} \sin^{2} \psi & -\kappa_{T} \sin \psi & (\kappa_{\perp} - \kappa_{\parallel}) \sin \psi \cos \psi \\
\kappa_{T} \sin \psi & \kappa_{\perp} & \kappa_{T} \cos \psi \\
(\kappa_{\perp} - \kappa_{\parallel}) \sin \psi \cos \psi & -\kappa_{T} \cos \psi & \kappa_{\perp} \cos^{2} \psi + \kappa_{\parallel} \sin^{2} \psi
\end{cases} (3.9)$$ On average, changes in the heliosphere over the solar cycle occur so slowly with respect to the transit time of galactic cosmic rays and the solar wind through it that a quasisteady state can be assumed. The explicit time dependence *OUp/Ot* in (3.6) is therefore neglected. Furthermore, the modulation cavity is assumed to be symmetrical about the polar axis, so that all quantities are independent of qS. Numerical solutions to such a steady-state, axisymetric three-dimensional transport equation were obtained by Dorman and Milovinova (1973), Dorman and Kobylinski (1973) and Fisk (1974). Several authors (Dorman and Milovinova, 1975; Cecchini and Quenby, 1975; Fisk, 1975a, 1976c; Moraal and Gleeson, 1975) have recently started to employ 3-D solutions, however a systematic application to observations has as yet not been completed. If the cosmic-ray differential density is also symmetric about the ecliptic plane, i.e. *0U/00 = 02 Up/O02= 0* at 0 = 90 ~ then (3.6) can be reduced to the spherical symmetric form $$\frac{1}{r^2} \frac{\partial}{\partial r} \left( r^2 V U_p - r^2 \kappa_{rr} \frac{\partial U_p}{\partial r} \right) - \frac{1}{3 r^2} \frac{\partial}{\partial r} \left( r^2 V \right) \frac{\partial}{\partial p} \left( p U_p \right) = 0$$
 (3.10) (e.g. Urch and Gleeson, 1972a). Notice that ~Crr is an effective radial but anisotropic diffusion coefficient resulting from the linear combination of ~:li and ~:• given in (3.9). Analytical solutions to (3.10) were obtained by Parker (1965, 1966), Dolginov and Toptygin (1967), Fisk and Axford (1969), and Webb (1975). In such solutions idealized interplanetary conditions are specified: the radial solar wind velocity is assumed to be constant and the effective radial diffusion coefficient Krr has restricted functional dependence on heliocentric radius r and momentum p. Fisk (1969) and Urch (1971) also solved (3.10) numerically, and these solutions have been employed by e.g. Gleeson and Urch (1971), Lezniak and Webber (1971), Goldstein *et al.* (1970 b) and Urch and Gleeson (1972 a, 1972 b). Most of the important physical phenomena of cosmic-ray modulation have been discovered by means of these numerical solutions. It should be noted that in the above-mentioned solutions the diffusion tensor K (or its appropriate simplification) must be chosen by trial and error to fit the observed modulated and galactic cosmic-ray spectra. Gleeson and Axford (1968 c) and Gleeson and Urch (1973) developed a very useful approximate solution from which the modulation parameters can be *directly calculated.* Firstly, Gleeson and Axford (1968b) solved (3.10) by an iterative technique. A feature of this solution was that the radial component of the streaming (Sp)r = 0 to order *VrfiCrr.* With the above-mentioned spherical symmetry conditions, it follows directly from (3.3) and (3.4) that *(Sp)r = 0*  implies $$CVU_p - \kappa_{\rm rr} \frac{\partial U_p}{\partial r} = 0. \tag{3.11}$$ This 'force-field' equation can be directly solved for *Up* (Gleeson and Axford 1968 c). However a more elegant solution results if one notes that F0(x,p)= *Up(x,p)/4z~p z*  where Fo is the omnidirectional (averaged over particle pitch angle) distribution function in (x, p) space (see Equations 4.1). In terms of Fo Equation (3.11) becomes (Gleeson and Urch, 1973) $$\frac{\partial F_0}{\partial r} + \frac{Vp}{3\kappa_{\rm rr}} \frac{\partial F_0}{\partial p} = 0, \qquad (3.12)$$ with solution $$F_0(r,p) = F_0(R,p^*) (3.13)$$ along contours given by $$\frac{\mathrm{d}p}{\mathrm{d}r} = \frac{Vp}{3\kappa_{\rm rr}}.\tag{3.14}$$ Here R denotes the specified boundary of the modulation cavity, and p\* (r, R, p) can be obtained by integration of (3.14). The force-field solution is a valid approximation above kinetic energies ~150 MeV nucleon -z, and under certain conditions (p\*-p) can be identified with the mean momentum change of the cosmic rays (cf. Gleeson and Urch, 1973). In the case where ~Crr can be separated in the form

