 beam propagation is needed to understand the impact of the instability broadening on the GeV-scale cascade emission. Also, it's essential to include the inverse Compton cooling in the beam distribution evolution equation to understand the long-term time evolution of the beam-wave system. # APPENDIX # A. DIFFUSION COEFFICIENTS The diffusion coefficients are given by $$
D _ {i j} = \pi e ^ {2} \int d ^ {3} \boldsymbol {k} W (\boldsymbol {k}, t) \frac {k _ {i} k _ {j}}{k ^ {2}} \delta (\boldsymbol {k} \cdot \boldsymbol {v} - \omega_ {p}), \tag {A1}
$$ where the unstable wave wavevector $\boldsymbol{k}=(k,\theta',\varphi')$ and the beam particles velocity $\boldsymbol{v}=(v=c(1-\frac{1}{2\gamma^{2}}),\theta,\varphi=0)$ are both defined in the spherical coordinates with the beam propagation axis being the z-axis. Because of the azimuth symmetry, we set $\varphi=0$ without losing the generality yielding $$
D _ {i j} = \pi e ^ {2} \int k ^ {2} d k \int d \cos \theta^ {\prime} \int d \varphi^ {\prime} W (\pmb {k}, t) \frac {k _ {i} k _ {j}}{k ^ {2}} \delta (k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}}) [ \sin \theta^ {\prime} \sin \theta \cos \varphi^ {\prime} + \cos \theta^ {\prime} \cos \theta ] - \omega_ {p}). \qquad \mathrm{(A2)}
$$ After transforming the delta function we get $$
D _ {i j} = \pi e ^ {2} \int k ^ {2} d k \int d \cos \theta^ {\prime} \int d \varphi^ {\prime} W (\boldsymbol {k}, t) \frac {k _ {i} k _ {j}}{k ^ {2}} \frac {\delta (\varphi^ {\prime} - \varphi_ {*} ^ {\prime})}{k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}}) \sin \theta^ {\prime} \sin \theta \sin \varphi_ {*} ^ {\prime}}, \tag {A3}
$$ where $\cos \varphi_{*}^{\prime} = \frac{\omega_p / (kc(1 - \frac{1}{2\gamma^2})) - \cos\theta' \cos\theta}{\sin\theta' \sin\theta}$ . $k_{i}$ is the projection of wave-vector $(\pmb{k} = k\sin \theta^{\prime}\cos \varphi^{\prime}\hat{\pmb{x}} + k\sin \theta^{\prime}\sin \varphi^{\prime}\hat{\pmb{y}} + k\cos \theta^{\prime}\hat{\pmb{z}})$ to the spatial direction $i$ . We have fixed the azimuth angle of the pair beam to zero ( $\varphi = 0$ ), therefore we have only the beam modulus momentum $(p)$ and the angler direction $\hat{\pmb{\theta}} = \cos \theta \hat{\pmb{x}} - \sin \theta \hat{\pmb{z}}$ . Based on this we find that $k_{p}$ is the modulus of the wave-vector and $k_{\theta} = \pmb{k} \cdot \hat{\pmb{\theta}} = k[\sin \theta^{\prime}\cos \theta\cos \varphi^{\prime} - \cos \theta^{\prime}\sin \theta]$ . Substituting the values of $k_{p}$ and $k_{\theta}$ and integrating over $\varphi'$ gives $$
\left\{ \begin{array}{l} D _ {p p} \\ D _ {p \theta} \\ D _ {\theta \theta} \end{array} \right\} = \pi \frac {m _ {e} \omega_ {p} ^ {2}}{n _ {e}} \int_ {\omega_ {p} / c} ^ {\infty} k ^ {2} d k \int_ {\cos \theta_ {1} ^ {\prime}} ^ {\cos \theta_ {2} ^ {\prime}} d \cos \theta^ {\prime} \frac {W (\boldsymbol {k})}{k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}}) \sqrt {(\cos \theta^ {\prime} - \cos \theta_ {1} ^ {\prime}) (\cos \theta_ {2} ^ {\prime} - \cos \theta^ {\prime})}} \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 1 \\ \xi \\ \xi^ {2} \end{array} \right\}, \qquad \text {(A4)}
$$ where $$
\xi = \sin \theta^ {\prime} \cos \varphi_ {*} ^ {\prime} \cos \theta - \cos \theta^ {\prime} \sin \theta = \frac {\cos \theta \frac {\omega_ {p}}{k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}})} - \cos \theta^ {\prime}}{\sin \theta}. \tag {A5}
$$ and the boundaries of $\cos\theta'$ are fixed by the condition $$
\left| \cos \varphi_ {*} ^ {\prime} \right| = \left| \frac {\omega_ {p} / (k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}})) - \cos \theta^ {\prime} \cos \theta}{\sin \theta^ {\prime} \sin \theta} \right| \leq 1, \tag {A6}
$$ which gives $$
\cos \theta_ {1, 2} ^ {\prime} = \frac {\omega_ {p}}{k c (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}})} \left[ \cos \theta \pm \sin \theta \sqrt {\left(\frac {k c}{\omega_ {p}}\right) ^ {2} (1 - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}}) ^ {2} - 1} \right]. \tag {A7}
$$ Since we have the calculations for the linear growth rate in the Cartesian coordinates $(k_{\perp}, \epsilon_{||})$ where $k_{||} = \frac{\omega_p}{c}(1 + \epsilon_{||})$ , we need to transform the diffusion coefficients integrand from the polar $(k, \cos \theta')$ to the Cartesian $(k_{\perp}, \epsilon_{||})$ . Multiplying eq.(A4) by the Jacobian determinant $|J| = \frac{\omega_p}{c} \frac{k_{\perp}}{k^2}$ , we get $$
\left\{ \begin{array}{l} D _ {p p} \\ D _ {p \theta} \\ D _ {\theta \theta} \end{array} \right\} = \pi \frac {m _ {e} \omega_ {p} ^ {2}}{n _ {e} c} \int d \epsilon_ {| |} \int_ {k _ {\perp , 1}} ^ {\infty} d k _ {\perp} k _ {\perp} \frac {W (k _ {\perp} , \epsilon_ {| |})}{\sqrt {\theta^ {2} \left(\frac {k _ {\perp}}{\omega_ {p} / c}\right) ^ {2} + \epsilon_ {| |} \left[ \theta^ {2} + \frac {1}{\gamma^ {2}} \right] - \epsilon_ {| |} ^ {2} - \left[ \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}} + \frac {\theta^ {2}}{2} \right] ^ {2}}} \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 1 \\ \xi \\ \xi^ {2} \end{array} \right\}, \qquad \text {(A8)}
$$ where for $\theta << 1$ and $\epsilon_{||} << 1$ , we can approximate $\xi$ as $$
\xi = - \frac {\omega_ {p}}{k c} \frac {1}{\theta} \left[ \frac {\theta^ {2}}{2} + \epsilon_ {| |} - \frac {1}{2 \gamma^ {2}} \right]. \tag {A9}
$$ The resonance boundaries translate to a lower bound on $k_{\perp}$ for a given $\epsilon_{||}$ .
